.TH FOPEN 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fopen, fdopen, freopen \- open a stream
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, \-lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "FILE *fopen(const char *" path ", const char *" mode );
.br
.BI "FILE *fdopen(int " fd ", const char *" mode );
.br
.BI "FILE *freopen(const char *" path ", const char *" mode ", FILE *" stream );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR fopen ()
function opens the file whose name is the string pointed to by
.I path
and associates a buffered I/O stream with it.  The newly created
.B FILE
object is fully buffered
.RB ( _IOFBF )
by default and is added to an internal list of open streams so that
.BR fflush (NULL)
and
.BR fcloseall ()
can act on it.
.PP
The
.I mode
argument is a null-terminated string beginning with one of the
following sequences:
.TP 8
.B r
Open for reading.  The stream is positioned at the beginning of the
file.  The file must exist.
.TP
.B w
Open for writing.  The file is created if it does not exist, and
truncated to zero length if it does.  The stream is positioned at the
beginning of the file.
.TP
.B a
Open for appending.  The file is created if it does not exist.  The
stream is positioned at the end of the file.
.PP
The initial mode character may be followed, in any order, by any of
the following modifier characters:
.TP 8
.B +
Open for both reading and writing
.RB ( O_RDWR ).
The initial position and truncation behaviour are determined by the
leading mode character.
.TP
.B x
(C11) Exclusive create.  Combined with
.B w
or
.BR a ,
this causes the underlying
.BR open (2)
call to specify
.BR O_EXCL ,
so the call fails if the file already exists.
.PP
Other characters that may appear in
.I mode
on conforming systems (such as
.BR b ,
.BR e ,
or
.BR m )
are silently ignored by the Substrate implementation; only the
characters listed above influence the resulting file descriptor flags.
.PP
The
.BR fdopen ()
function associates a stream with the existing file descriptor
.IR fd .
The
.I mode
argument is parsed as for
.BR fopen ()
but is used only to record the access flags on the
.B FILE
object; it does not change the underlying file descriptor and must be
compatible with the access mode of
.IR fd .
After a successful call,
.I fd
is owned by the stream and will be closed by
.BR fclose (3).
.PP
The
.BR freopen ()
function reuses the
.B FILE
object pointed to by
.I stream
to open the file named by
.IR path .
Any pending output is first flushed and the existing descriptor is
closed; the stream's buffer, error indicator, EOF indicator, and
push-back state are then reset, and the new file is opened with the
flags derived from
.IR mode .
.BR freopen ()
is typically used to redirect one of the predefined streams
.RB ( stdin ,
.BR stdout ,
.BR stderr )
to a file.
.SS Substrate Implementation
The Substrate
.BR fopen ()
recognises only the leading mode characters
.BR r ,
.BR w ,
and
.B a
and the modifiers
.B +
and
.BR x ;
any other leading character causes
.B NULL
to be returned without setting
.I errno
to a portable value.
.PP
.BR freopen ()
in the current revision does not support a
.B NULL
.I path
argument: the C99 mode-change variant always returns
.B NULL
because the implementation does not yet use
.BR fcntl (2)
.B F_SETFL
to alter the flags of an already-open descriptor.
.PP
.BR fdopen ()
always allocates an internal buffer of
.B BUFSIZ
bytes regardless of
.IR fd 's
underlying type; callers that require unbuffered or line-buffered
behaviour must use
.BR setvbuf (3)
afterwards.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR fopen (),
.BR fdopen (),
and
.BR freopen ()
return a pointer to the new
.B FILE
object.  On failure they return
.BR NULL .
.PP
On error from
.BR fopen ()
or
.BR freopen ()
the underlying
.BR open (2)
call sets
.I errno
to indicate the cause; on a memory-allocation failure inside
.BR fdopen ()
or
.BR fopen ()
.I errno
is set to
.B ENOMEM
by the allocator.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
The
.I mode
argument supplied to
.BR fopen ()
or
.BR freopen ()
does not begin with
.BR r ,
.BR w ,
or
.BR a .
(The Substrate implementation reports this by returning
.B NULL
without invoking
.BR open (2);
.I errno
may not be modified in that case.)
.TP
.B EBADF
The descriptor passed to
.BR fdopen ()
is negative.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
Insufficient memory to allocate the
.B FILE
object or its buffer.
.PP
In addition,
.BR fopen ()
and
.BR freopen ()
may fail and set
.I errno
to any of the values specified for
.BR open (2),
including
.BR EACCES ,
.BR EEXIST
(when the
.B x
modifier is used),
.BR EISDIR ,
.BR ELOOP ,
.BR EMFILE ,
.BR ENAMETOOLONG ,
.BR ENFILE ,
.BR ENOENT ,
.BR ENOSPC ,
.BR ENOTDIR ,
.BR EOVERFLOW ,
and
.BR EROFS .
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program opens a file for writing, truncating it if it
already exists, and writes a single line of text:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int
main(void)
{
    FILE *fp = fopen("/tmp/hello.txt", "w");
    if (fp == NULL) {
        perror("fopen");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    fprintf(fp, "hello, substrate\en");
    fclose(fp);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The next example uses the C11
.B x
modifier to fail rather than overwrite an existing file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <errno.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int
main(void)
{
    FILE *fp = fopen("/tmp/once.lock", "wx");
    if (fp == NULL) {
        if (errno == EEXIST)
            fprintf(stderr, "already locked\en");
        else
            fprintf(stderr, "fopen: %s\en", strerror(errno));
        return 1;
    }
    fclose(fp);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The following snippet wraps an existing file descriptor obtained from
.BR pipe (2)
in a stream using
.BR fdopen ():
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int
main(void)
{
    int pfd[2];
    if (pipe(pfd) < 0) return 1;

    FILE *r = fdopen(pfd[0], "r");
    FILE *w = fdopen(pfd[1], "w");
    if (!r || !w) return 1;

    fputs("through the pipe\en", w);
    fflush(w);

    char buf[64];
    if (fgets(buf, sizeof(buf), r))
        fputs(buf, stdout);

    fclose(w);
    fclose(r);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Finally,
.BR freopen ()
is used to redirect
.B stdout
to a log file for the remainder of the program:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    if (freopen("/tmp/program.log", "w", stdout) == NULL) {
        perror("freopen");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("this line goes to the log file\en");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR open (2),
.BR pipe (2),
.BR fclose (3),
.BR fflush (3),
.BR fread (3),
.BR fwrite (3),
.BR setvbuf (3),
.BR stdio (3)
.SH STANDARDS
.BR fopen ()
and
.BR freopen ()
conform to C89, C99, C11, and POSIX.1-2008.  The
.B x
mode modifier was added in C11.
.BR fdopen ()
conforms to POSIX.1-2008.
.PP
The behaviour of
.BR freopen ()
when invoked with a
.B NULL
.I path
(introduced in C99 to permit changing the access mode of an already
open stream) is not implemented in the current Substrate revision and
returns
.BR NULL .
.SH COLOPHON
